---
// Lesson 11.2b — Create PT: Submit as Final
// hybrid CPT: Digital Portfolio submission; final checklist; celebration.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>11.2b — Create PT: Submit as Final</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Today the Create PT becomes COLLEGE BOARD PROPERTY. You will
    open the Digital Portfolio, upload the program, the video, and
    the written responses, and press the button your teacher
    cannot press for you. Then — a unit's worth of weight off —
    we pivot to the exam itself.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity hybrid">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Submission</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~35 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>What goes in the Digital Portfolio (all five, in
    order):</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Program code (the .zip or the single file):</strong>
            your whole project — including the lines your PPR
            cites, verbatim</li>
        <li><strong>Video:</strong> your sub-60s one take
            (<a href="11.2a.html">11.2a</a>)</li>
        <li><strong>WR1:</strong> purpose + abstraction</li>
        <li><strong>WR2a / WR2b / WR2c:</strong> algorithm,
            development story, list — copy carefully from your
            <a href="11.1e.html">11.1e</a> drafts into the
            Portfolio's text boxes (they paste PLAIN text — check
            formatting after pasting)</li>
        <li><strong>PPR:</strong> uploaded as your exam-day
            reference</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>The final final check — read aloud with a
    partner:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>WR text in the Portfolio == WR text in your plan
            (pasting drops things — VERIFY, do not trust)</li>
        <li>Every code excerpt in the PPR exists verbatim in the
            submitted program</li>
        <li>Video plays from the Portfolio preview (not just your
            disk)</li>
        <li>No names, no school, no faces without permission
            forms</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Then <strong>Submit as Final.</strong> The deadline is
    April 30, 11:59 PM ET — you are submitting EARLY, because
    upload failures happen and the Portfolio does not care whose
    fault. Take the confirmation screenshot (yes, really) and put
    it in your repo: <code>commit "11.2b: SUBMITTED"</code>.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Portfolio traps</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>The Portfolio says "draft" even after you think
                you submitted. Which button did you miss —
                and why is a screenshot the only proof?</li>
            <li>Your WR2b tells the debugging story of a bug you
                fixed in the version you did NOT submit. What
                must be true about WR2b's relationship to the
                final code?</li>
            <li>Classmate submits your shared jam code. Why is
                your submission YOUR fork of the work — and what
                does the Academic Integrity policy say about
                "the same algorithm with different names"
                (answer: plagiarism is about UNATTRIBUTED
                reuse; your year's forks were LICENSED reuse,
                <a href="9.10.html">9.10</a> — but the PT
                you submit must be your authorship)</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity hybrid">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Pivot</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>The exam is next.</strong> 30% of your score just
    went to College Board. The remaining 70% is May 14, 8:00 AM,
    Session 1, in Bluebook: 70 multiple-choice questions (120
    minutes) and 4 written responses (60 minutes) — WR1/WR2 on the
    END-OF-COURSE exam are DIFFERENT prompts than your PT ones;
    you bring your PPR for WR2. Everything from
    <a href="9.1.html">9.1</a> to here is now MC practice
    material.</p>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Breathe, then sharpen</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your exam-prep plan into the repo: which
                <a href="../exam/mc-strategy.html">MC strategy</a>
                gaps to drill first, based on your honest guess
                (data? networks? pseudocode tracing?)</li>
            <li>Commit + push: <code>"11.2b: PT submitted + pivot to exam prep"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>See the Portfolio confirmation (screenshot in repo)</li>
            <li>Verify all five artifacts match each other</li>
            <li>State what is done: 30% of the score, banked</li>
            <li>State what remains: May 14, 8 AM, Bluebook, 70 MC + 4 WR</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../exam/index.html">Exam Hub</a> — dates, format, everything</li>
        <li><a href="../exam/written-responses.html">Written Responses</a> — exam-day WR vs PT WR</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="11.3.html">11.3 — Written-Response Practice (PPR in hand)</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="11.2b" title="11.2b Create PT: Submit as Final — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
